A complete multi-layer audit found no fault in the live lab. Every diagnostic phase returned clean results: ICMP reachability between all host pairs is 0% loss; no nftables/iptables drop rules exist on any device; all L2 identities are unique (no duplicate MACs); no static ARP poison entries; all five OSPF/FRR routers hold Full adjacencies with nine routes each; no tc/qdisc shaping is applied on any of the 30 interfaces examined; all four web servers (web_server_0–3 at 10.200.0.3–.6) respond HTTP 200 to direct curl requests from both host_1_1_1_1 and host_2_1_1_1 in ~4 ms; DNS resolves web0.local–web3.local to the correct IPs via dns_server (10.200.0.2) from both clients; and no device shows elevated CPU, socket count, or stress-tool activity. The user's report of "two sites vanishing at the same time" is unconfirmed by any current evidence and leaves no historical trace — no carrier-change counters, no daemon log entries, no residual ACL or route anomaly. Root cause: no anomaly exists in the lab at the time of inspection. Fix: no corrective action is needed; the lab is operating correctly on all layers. If the outage recurs, capture the symptom while active (HTTP status codes, curl output, `ip route get`, FRR neighbor state) to distinguish a transient service crash from a routing or link event.
